{"name":"Comet Tail","hex":"#c1daed","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"comet tail","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A display artifact consisting of a smear of color that appears to follow a moving object in the picture.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see comet,‎ tail."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/comet_tail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Comet tail","blocks":[{"text":"A comet tail is a projection of material from a comet that often becomes visible when illuminated by the Sun, while the comet passes through the inner Solar System. As a comet approaches the Sun, solar radiation causes the volatile materials within the comet to vaporize and stream out of the comet nucleus, carrying dust away with them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Comet_Parts.svg/330px-Comet_Parts.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comet_tail","wikidata":"Q559875"},{"title":"Comet","blocks":[{"text":"A comet is an icy, small Solar System body or interstellar object that warms and begins to release gases when passing close to the Sun, a process called outgassing. This produces an extended, gravitationally unbound atmosphere or coma surrounding the nucleus, and sometimes a tail of gas and dust gas blown out from the coma. These phenomena are due to the effects of solar radiation and the outstreaming solar wind plasma acting upon the nucleus of the comet. Comet nuclei range from a few hundred meters to tens of kilometers across and are composed of loose collections of ice, dust, and small rocky particles. The coma may be up to 15 times Earth's diameter, while the tail may stretch beyond one astronomical unit. If sufficiently close and bright, a comet may be seen from Earth without the aid of a telescope and can subtend an arc of up to 30° across the sky. Comets have been observed and recorded since ancient times by many cultures and religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Comet_Hale-Bopp_1995O1.jpg/330px-Comet_Hale-Bopp_1995O1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comet","wikidata":"Q3559"},{"title":"Tail","blocks":[{"text":"The tail is the elongated section at the rear end of a bilaterian animal's body; in general, the term refers to a distinct, flexible appendage extending backwards from the midline of the torso. In vertebrate animals that evolved to lose their tails, the coccyx is the homologous vestigial of the tail. While tails are primarily considered a feature of vertebrates, some invertebrates such as scorpions and springtails, as well as snails and slugs, have tail-like appendages that are also referred to as tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg/330px-White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail","wikidata":"Q60960"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Comet_A3_2023_Tsuchinshan-Atlas.jpg/500px-Comet_A3_2023_Tsuchinshan-Atlas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Comet_A3_2023_Tsuchinshan-Atlas.jpg/1280px-Comet_A3_2023_Tsuchinshan-Atlas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Comet_A3_2023_Tsuchinshan-Atlas.jpg/500px-Comet_A3_2023_Tsuchinshan-Atlas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComet_A3_2023_Tsuchinshan-Atlas.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Comet_and_tail_animation.gif/500px-Comet_and_tail_animation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Comet_and_tail_animation.gif/500px-Comet_and_tail_animation.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Animation of a comet's tail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComet_and_tail_animation.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Comet_Lemmon_Oct_30.png/500px-Comet_Lemmon_Oct_30.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Comet_Lemmon_Oct_30.png/1280px-Comet_Lemmon_Oct_30.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Comet_Lemmon_Oct_30.png/500px-Comet_Lemmon_Oct_30.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Comet C/2025 A6 (Lemmon) with its tail as seen in October 2025","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComet_Lemmon_Oct_30.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Arend-RolandPerifocal19570428Labelled.jpg/500px-Arend-RolandPerifocal19570428Labelled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Arend-RolandPerifocal19570428Labelled.jpg/500px-Arend-RolandPerifocal19570428Labelled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Finson-Probstein diagram for comet C/1956 R1 (Arend-Roland) on 28 April 1957 looking down on the orbital plane","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArend-RolandPerifocal19570428Labelled.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/ArendRoland19570428Labelled.jpg/500px-ArendRoland19570428Labelled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikiped